.TH DTREE 1
.SH NAME
dtree \- print the tree structure of a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dtree
[
.B \-adfglnpsvx
] [
.B \-c
length ] [ dir ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dtree
prints a directory as a tree structure, with the branches on the right
of the page.
If
.I dir
is specified, it is used as the root of the tree to print;
the default is to use the current directory.
The current date is output as
the first line.
.PP
The following options are allowed, and may be given in any order:
.TP 6
.B \-a
include files as well as directories in listing
.TP
.B \-d
sort tree with directories at the top
.TP
.B \-f
sort tree with files at the top
.TP
.B \-g
same as
.BR \-l ,
but use group name instead of user name
.TP
.B \-l
Print stats with each listing.
If both
.B \-g
and
.B \-l
flags are given,
both owner and group will be printed.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not sort the tree.
.TP
.B \-p
include files starting with a `.' (except `.' and `..').
.TP
.B \-s
Use shorter stats.
Implies
.B \-l
if
.B \-g
isn't given.
.TP
.B \-v
Variable length columns.
.TP
.B \-x
Do not cross mounted file systems.
.TP
.B \-c
Set max column length to be
.IR length .
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.2i
/etc/passwd	to get user ID's for \fBdtree \-l\fR.
/etc/group	to get group ID's for \fBdtree \-g\fR.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
date(1), pwd(1), stat(2), getgrent(3), dir(4), types(5)
.SH AUTHOR
Courtesy of St. Olaf College.
.sp
